  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Solve the equation ${2}^{z}=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{2}$ as ${2}^{-1}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{z} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {2}^{z} & ={2}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{z} & ={2}^{-1} \\\\ z & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z=-1.$",
